  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Sunday) \nIssued at 208 AM MDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nA dangerous surge of monsoon moisture is forecast to produce \nwidespread heavy rainfall over the forecast area Tuesday through \nThursday with a very good chance for scattered occurrences of \nflash flooding, as well as some main stem river and tributary \nflooding. The aforementioned CA low pressure system and a mid- \nlevel high pressure system parked over eastern TX will draw very \nrich subtropical moisture northeastward mainly along and east of \nthe continental divide; even as the upper low opens into a trough \nwhile passing northeastward over the Great Basin and northern \nRockies Tuesday and Tuesday night. Wednesday and Wednesday night, \na disturbance embedded in the monsoon flow will track \nnortheastward across the forecast area. Thursday and Thursday \nnight, another upper level disturbance will eject northeastward \nacross the Four Corners from the Baja Peninsula. Due to this \nactive subtropical moisture fetch, GEFS Ensemble PWATs spike up to\n200% of normal Tuesday through Thursday, except up to 250% of \nnormal Wednesday, with near record (if not record) PWATs likely. \nThe most likely rain amount Tuesday afternoon through Thursday is \ncurrently forecast to be 3-4\" from Ruidoso and Roswell northward \nto around Las Vegas, Roy, and Clayton, except locally around 5\" \nover parts of east central NM. Amounts around 1-3\" are forecast \nwestward to the continental divide, with lighter amounts west of \nthere. The NBM 90th Percentile (serves as an upper-end scenario \nfor planning purposes) indicates a 10 percent chance that some \nlocations along and east of the central mountain chain, and in the\nlower Rio Grande Valley, may accumulate around 6-8\" of rain, with\n3-6\" westward to the continental divide. With this forecast \npackage we issued a Flash Flood Watch for much of the area along \nand east of the continental divide Tuesday afternoon through \nWednesday, except for the far eastern plains where the Watch will \nprobably be expanded as later shifts extend it in time through \nWednesday night...and probably eventually Thursday. \n\nRain amounts should trend downward on Friday, but there are signs\nof yet another disturbance tracking eastward over the central and\nsouthern Rockies. Models depict PWATs beginning to trend downward\nFriday, while remaining up to 160% of normal in the wake of \nThursday's disturbance. This will be plenty of moisture for \nlocally heavy rainfall to occur and produce more flooding \nproblems Friday afternoon and evening, especially given all of \nthe antecedent moisture in place. Over the weekend drier air will\nfilter over the forecast area from the west, while the upper high\nmigrates southwestward over northern Mexico and a ridge axis\nbuilds northward over NM. This will cause a sharp downtick in \nstorm coverage and rainfall intensity.\n\nTemperature-wise, except for some near and above average highs\nover central and southeast areas today, readings across the \nforecast area for the remainder of the work week will vary from a\nfew to several degrees below 30-year averages. Highs should \nbottom out on Wednesday around 10-20 degrees below 30-year \naverages, which will place them mostly in the 60s to low 70s, \nexcept for some 50s and upper 40s in the mountains. Readings will \nrebound over the weekend.",
